1
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:12,330
Greetings and welcome to the lecture on standard operating procedures.

2
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:17,530
Standard operating procedures represent an administrative control.

3
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:22,230
This lecture module has been designed and developed to introduce you to the basic concepts

4
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:26,350
of design and development of standard operating procedures.

5
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:33,550
It is intended for beginners.

6
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:38,730
In this lecture module, I will introduce you to the concept of the SOP.

7
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:45,130
I will introduce you to the process of development of an SOP and provide guidance on the development

8
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:52,640
of your own specific SOPs.

9
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:57,080
Upon completion of this module, you should demonstrate the ability to describe the function

10
00:00:57,080 --> 00:01:02,720
of an SOP, describe the procedure of design, development, implementation and improvement

11
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:12,180
of an SOP, and design and develop a basic standard operating procedure.

12
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:18,740
The goal of an SOP is to ensure reproducibility.

13
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:24,920
What this means is that an SOP ensures that different individuals performing the same

14
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:28,980
procedure obtain a similar result.

15
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:34,740
This is very important in laboratory facilities which are involved in routine diagnostics and

16
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:35,740
testing.

17
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:44,510
We have a responsibility to the end users as well as to the patients whose samples we

18
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:54,360
are analyzing and adherence to an SOP ensures that the results are reproducible.

19
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:58,680
This is a graphical representation of what I have just mentioned.

20
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:04,880
In any laboratory facility, we have different individual laboratory workers.

21
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:11,040
Each of them has their own knowledge, skills and abilities.

22
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:19,520
They conduct a same process using an SOP and ideally a good SOP should result in a

23
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:28,290
same output for the same result.

24
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:33,650
This is what might happen if your SOP has not been developed and designed in accordance

25
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:36,610
with a standard procedure.

26
00:02:36,610 --> 00:02:42,850
You will have different laboratory workers conducting the same process, performing the

27
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:46,650
same operations and you will have different results.

28
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:53,370
In this case, your SOP must be revised and this is part of the performance assessment

29
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:56,530
which I will discuss in the following week.

30
00:02:56,530 --> 00:03:05,830
Now, an SOP is an instructional document.

31
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:14,240
It is like a prescription which is reader centered and it is very important to engage

32
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:18,240
the end users during the development of the SOP.

33
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:24,920
As a BioRisk Manager, you must obtain input from the end users prior to formulating or

34
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:30,560
developing the final version of the standard operating procedure.

35
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:37,600
So the key point is instructional document which implies that it states a clear set

36
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:44,840
of instructions which can be achieved, which are measurable and which can be evaluated

37
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:54,100
after completion for performance and other issues.

38
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:01,820
The instructional document teaches a reader to understand a rule or principle, envision

39
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:08,660
a process or workflow, perform a task and use a tool.

40
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:16,620
What this means is that the SOP should be very clear in terms of its context and in

41
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:20,260
terms of the step-by-step implementation.

42
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:27,660
The word envision refers to the ability of an individual to visualize the process.

43
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:33,580
So the process must be comprehensible to the reader or to the laboratory worker as

44
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:35,940
the case may be.

45
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:41,900
They should be able to apply the step-by-step procedure to perform the task or to use a

46
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:47,580
tool as in the case of a laboratory, this may be a specific piece of equipment.

47
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:58,060
Let us now move on to the process of development of an SOP.

48
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:03,700
The process of development of an SOP depends on the process of analysis.

49
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:11,140
So we first analyze a specific procedure, we design a set of steps around that particular

50
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:18,780
procedure, we develop these steps and translate them into a document, we implement these steps

51
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:25,780
at the laboratory level and we evaluate the procedure for effectiveness and consistency

52
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:27,620
of operation.

53
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:35,390
This is the basic procedure for SOP development.

54
00:05:35,390 --> 00:05:42,670
Now an instructional document or an SOP is based on certain other prerequisites.

55
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:50,390
An SOP is generally based on a policy which is a plan or guiding principle that influences

56
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:51,750
other action.

57
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:56,170
So this is the parent of the SOP which is the policy.

58
00:05:56,170 --> 00:06:02,070
The policy then gets translated into a program plan which is a set of tasks or actions

59
00:06:02,070 --> 00:06:09,070
performed in a specified sequence or manner that achieves a particular result.

60
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:13,510
And finally, the program plan is translated into a procedure.

61
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:20,310
A procedure is a specific task, work or instruction or action and procedures may include actions

62
00:06:20,310 --> 00:06:32,200
as well as other specific requirements.

63
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:40,760
A policy document is written with a certain audience in mind, we always ask who the

64
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:46,160
audience is, who is the end user of the SOP or the policy document, who writes the

65
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:48,240
document, the authors of the document.

66
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:54,400
This may be the biarisk manager as well as certain experts in that field and the intended

67
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,400
purpose of the SOP.

68
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:02,800
SOP has a very specific purpose and this must be clearly defined in the scope and objectives

69
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:04,440
of the SOP.

70
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:10,800
For instance, we will be looking at the process of disposal of biological waste in compliance

71
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:18,600
with national regulations for the purpose of this particular lecture module.

72
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:20,680
So I have answered those questions.

73
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:25,360
So in the case of this particular document, who writes this document?

74
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:29,960
It's the biarisk manager in consultation with the laboratory workers and management.

75
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:31,920
Who is the audience?

76
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:34,880
Laboratory workers and external contractors.

77
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:42,000
Now I have used this specific term external contractor because in most containment laboratories,

78
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:48,080
the process of disposal of biological waste or chemical waste is done via a third party

79
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:50,200
or an external contractor.

80
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:58,880
And this external contractor must be fully cognizant with regard to the SOP.

81
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:03,640
And what is the intended purpose of the SOP is basically to ensure the safe disposal of

82
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:08,320
biological waste in compliance with national regulations.

83
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:15,840
This comes down to our original intent or our original title.

84
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:20,840
Now in order to develop your SOP, you must visualize the process.

85
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,880
You should envision the entire process.

86
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:28,440
Now this is a process is envisioned very simplistically.

87
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:35,480
So we have biological waste, which is being produced by a facility on a daily basis.

88
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:40,240
And this biological waste must be segregated at the facility itself in order to ensure

89
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,760
that it is disposed of safely.

90
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:51,080
So we can dispose of the waste after segregation as radioactive waste, biological waste or

91
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:54,040
chemical waste as the case may be.

92
00:08:54,040 --> 00:09:00,760
Now in order for the transporter or the contractor to identify this particular waste, the laboratory

93
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:07,920
workers must ensure that the waste is labeled appropriately, following which the waste is

94
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:14,960
transported to another facility for disposal and incineration as the case may be.

95
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:23,760
Now most of the biological waste within a containment facility is autoclaved and incinerated.

96
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:28,360
Autoclaving must be conducted or carried out at the facility itself.

97
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:36,280
And incineration may have to be carried out at another facility, as this involves specific

98
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:40,720
equipment such as incinerators.

99
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:46,760
Radioactive waste and other waste such as chemical waste, which may be components of the biological

100
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:54,590
waste must also be treated in compliance with national regulations and guidelines.

101
00:09:54,590 --> 00:10:02,900
So this is basically our program plan for our SOP development.

102
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:07,100
So the program plan designates specific steps.

103
00:10:07,100 --> 00:10:12,300
So we have the procedure for collection of waste, procedure for segregation of waste,

104
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:19,460
transport of waste, decontamination and neutralization of waste, procedure for labels and signage,

105
00:10:19,460 --> 00:10:24,140
procedure for training of personnel handling biological waste and the personnel protective

106
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:28,740
equipment which must be worn when managing biological waste.

107
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:35,780
Now all of these represent separate SOPs in the program plan for the disposal of

108
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:44,140
biological waste.

109
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:47,780
The sections of the SOP are as follows.

110
00:10:47,780 --> 00:10:58,260
An SOP has conditions, context, specific actions and documentation.

111
00:10:58,260 --> 00:11:04,740
Let us look at these sections one by one.

112
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:08,660
The conditions for the SOP should answer these following questions.

113
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:10,080
Who should use the SOP?

114
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:12,760
When should it be used?

115
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:14,640
Why should it be used?

116
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:17,360
And where should it be used?

117
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:20,140
An SOP is a very specific document.

118
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:26,720
It cannot be adapted for use to other situations or if it is adapted, it should undergo the

119
00:11:26,720 --> 00:11:30,160
process of implementation and validation.

120
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:36,600
This is why these specific questions must be established when we set up the conditions

121
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:42,240
for the development of the SOP.

122
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:51,560
In this case of disposal of laboratory waste, the users of the SOP are the laboratory workers.

123
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:55,980
It is used during the disposal of laboratory waste.

124
00:11:55,980 --> 00:12:01,480
It is used for the safe disposal of biological waste and it should be used at the containment

125
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:13,210
facility, transport and disposal facility as defined by the respective SOPs.

126
00:12:13,210 --> 00:12:18,170
The next aspect of SOP writing involves establishment of context.

127
00:12:18,170 --> 00:12:22,250
So the context involves a basic process in which we have an input.

128
00:12:22,250 --> 00:12:25,450
In this case, we have biological waste as an input.

129
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:34,370
The actions which are used to treat that biological waste or mitigate the risk of exposure

130
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:40,490
of laboratory personnel and the external environment to that biological waste.

131
00:12:40,490 --> 00:12:46,250
So we have specific actions which are listed in the SOP and the output which is the intended

132
00:12:46,250 --> 00:12:48,530
outcome of that particular SOP.

133
00:12:48,530 --> 00:12:59,560
When you establish context for an SOP, please ensure that you include preparation.

134
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:03,640
Preparation assumes readiness before implementation of the SOP.

135
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:11,160
For instance, what this implies is that specific procedures must be in place prior

136
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:15,720
to the implementation of the SOP for the disposal of waste.

137
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:22,040
This may include training of the laboratory personnel with regard to the segregation of

138
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:30,440
the waste into biological, chemical and other components.

139
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:34,240
Now we are developing this SOP based on the following context.

140
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:42,440
We develop the SOP assuming that personnel are trained, that they are competent, that

141
00:13:42,440 --> 00:13:48,840
facilities for storage are available, facilities for segregation exist, facilities for transportation

142
00:13:48,840 --> 00:13:53,240
exist and facilities for decontamination exist.

143
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:55,560
This is an assumption.

144
00:13:55,560 --> 00:14:03,040
Now if any of these following measures do not exist, we must develop an SOP to fill

145
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:09,670
in the gap.

146
00:14:09,670 --> 00:14:15,230
The actions are specific steps which must be taken to move from the input to the

147
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:17,790
output.

148
00:14:17,790 --> 00:14:25,230
As I mentioned earlier, an SOP is reader-centric, so the steps should be very clearly stated

149
00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:31,750
and should not be ambiguous.

150
00:14:31,750 --> 00:14:36,310
These are some of the steps or the actions which must be incorporated in the SOP for

151
00:14:36,310 --> 00:14:41,390
waste disposal and I have stated here checklist.

152
00:14:41,390 --> 00:14:47,670
Now an SOP can be translated into a checklist with a step and a check mark.

153
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:53,230
So the steps as follows, place all the waste from the facility into the designated container.

154
00:14:53,230 --> 00:14:59,790
So I check, label the containers with the designated labels, check and transfer the

155
00:14:59,790 --> 00:15:05,510
containers to the staging areas checked and inform the shipper when 50% of the staging

156
00:15:05,510 --> 00:15:09,750
area has been occupied, checked and so on and so forth.

157
00:15:09,750 --> 00:15:17,230
So these actions can be clearly defined and once the action has been completed, the

158
00:15:17,230 --> 00:15:29,110
BiRisk Manager can check the SOP for implementation.

159
00:15:29,110 --> 00:15:36,350
In addition to the SOP itself and the checklist, documentation must be incorporated into the

160
00:15:36,350 --> 00:15:39,830
SOP and included in the appendices.

161
00:15:39,830 --> 00:15:47,710
This documentation includes cross-references such as guidelines and best practices, regulatory

162
00:15:47,710 --> 00:15:53,790
documentation pertaining to national laws on disposal of biological waste, as well as

163
00:15:53,790 --> 00:16:04,530
specific regional regulations.

164
00:16:04,530 --> 00:16:11,330
An SOP must be implemented and this provides a testing bed for the SOP.

165
00:16:11,330 --> 00:16:20,610
The SOP is tested at the level of the laboratory workbench and these are some of the questions

166
00:16:20,610 --> 00:16:25,850
which a BiRisk Manager must pose to the end users of the SOP.

167
00:16:25,850 --> 00:16:29,330
The first one concerns comprehension.

168
00:16:29,330 --> 00:16:34,770
We must ask our end users if they understand the SOP.

169
00:16:34,770 --> 00:16:40,730
In some cases, the language used in the SOP may be too technical or too complicated for

170
00:16:40,730 --> 00:16:48,810
an end user to comprehend and comprehension is critical in order to minimize errors.

171
00:16:49,570 --> 00:16:53,050
An SOP should not be ambiguous.

172
00:16:53,050 --> 00:16:56,250
The next aspect involves implementation.

173
00:16:56,250 --> 00:17:03,010
We must inquire with the end user if they could physically do what the SOP asks, if

174
00:17:03,010 --> 00:17:06,010
no, why.

175
00:17:06,010 --> 00:17:12,290
For instance, if you have provided a laboratory worker with a biosafety suit which is not

176
00:17:12,290 --> 00:17:18,970
of the appropriate size, he or she may not be able to wear it or if they do wear it,

177
00:17:18,970 --> 00:17:23,210
they may not be able to perform the operation because the suit is too tight.

178
00:17:23,210 --> 00:17:32,490
So in this case, an observation will provide the BiRisk Manager with the appropriate input.

179
00:17:32,490 --> 00:17:35,770
The next aspect is reproducibility.

180
00:17:35,770 --> 00:17:42,610
This comes back to different laboratory users achieving the same outcome and if no, why

181
00:17:45,050 --> 00:17:48,170
The next aspect is consistency.

182
00:17:48,170 --> 00:17:54,210
And this is critical when we do diagnostic testing in the laboratory as errorless results

183
00:17:54,210 --> 00:18:02,570
can lead to different diagnosis and can affect the stakeholders and end users.

184
00:18:02,570 --> 00:18:15,030
So all of these aspects must be documented during the process of implementation of an SOP.

185
00:18:15,110 --> 00:18:21,830
Another aspect of SOP development involves validation and in validation we have what

186
00:18:21,830 --> 00:18:26,070
is known as behavioral observation data.

187
00:18:26,070 --> 00:18:32,550
A behavioral observation data is basically a questionnaire which we provide to the laboratory

188
00:18:32,550 --> 00:18:39,390
worker or the end user and all that he or she has to do is answer it as a yes or a

189
00:18:39,390 --> 00:18:40,390
no.

190
00:18:40,390 --> 00:18:46,950
There is no ambiguous answer for a DoD questionnaire.

191
00:18:46,950 --> 00:18:52,230
For example, does X don his biosafety suit in compliance with the SOP?

192
00:18:52,230 --> 00:18:53,830
Yes or no?

193
00:18:53,830 --> 00:18:59,310
For example, if the answer is no, then we investigate Y.

194
00:18:59,310 --> 00:19:04,870
If the answer is yes, we continue with that particular SOP.

195
00:19:04,870 --> 00:19:14,120
So these are very useful in validating the SOPs from the perspective of the end user.

196
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:19,160
So DoDs help us to check and validate SOP.

197
00:19:19,160 --> 00:19:29,040
They are an objective assessment as an observer basically observes the laboratory worker and

198
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:31,640
collects data.

199
00:19:31,640 --> 00:19:41,120
The observer himself improves as the observer notes the discrepancies between the SOP statements

200
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:44,520
as well as the implementation plan.

201
00:19:44,520 --> 00:19:49,160
And finally, the DoD allows us to communicate across the group.

202
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:55,960
For instance, if a specific laboratory worker cannot achieve a specific objective and obtain

203
00:19:55,960 --> 00:20:02,280
the reproducible result, it may be an indication of the failure of the system.

204
00:20:02,280 --> 00:20:11,420
And this can be addressed by communicating across the organization.

205
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:17,380
So DoD data must involve personnel who will be observed and developing the DoD questionnaire.

206
00:20:17,380 --> 00:20:25,060
For instance, if I am the biorisk manager and laboratory worker is involved in a specific

207
00:20:25,060 --> 00:20:32,740
procedure, I will involve him or her during the process of development of the questionnaire

208
00:20:32,740 --> 00:20:39,780
as they are more aware of certain factors which the biorisk manager may not be able

209
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:43,170
to observe.

210
00:20:43,170 --> 00:20:50,570
The ability also ensures consistency and it provides an opportunity for self-audit which

211
00:20:50,570 --> 00:20:59,880
is more realistic than an audit done by a third party.

212
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:03,560
Now SOPs in general must be approved.

213
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:09,240
And this procedure of approval may involve several rounds of discussion and we may have

214
00:21:09,240 --> 00:21:18,040
consultative sessions with the biorisk managers, even the top management and the case stakeholders.

215
00:21:18,040 --> 00:21:26,120
So once an SOP has been designed and developed, it is tested in the lab, implemented and finally

216
00:21:26,120 --> 00:21:34,990
an approval is obtained which legalizes the SOP at the level of the organization.

217
00:21:34,990 --> 00:21:42,910
SOPs are living documents, they have to be revised and reviewed constantly and the review

218
00:21:42,910 --> 00:21:52,430
is dependent on various factors such as near miss, accidents or incidents.

219
00:21:52,430 --> 00:21:58,110
If you experience accidents or incidents in your laboratory when implementing a specific

220
00:21:58,190 --> 00:22:03,390
SOP, the SOP will have to be reviewed.

221
00:22:03,390 --> 00:22:11,150
You may also have to review an SOP when the results are inconsistent.

222
00:22:11,150 --> 00:22:23,310
This SOP must be revised and reviewed periodically.

223
00:22:23,310 --> 00:22:29,470
So prior to review of an SOP, we must check the documentation and determine whether there

224
00:22:29,470 --> 00:22:34,110
has been any sign that it has been previously reviewed or revised.

225
00:22:34,110 --> 00:22:40,950
If there are too many revisions, it may be indicative of the failure in certain steps

226
00:22:40,950 --> 00:22:43,430
of the SOP.

227
00:22:43,430 --> 00:22:51,030
And a review must be undertaken in consultation with the end users.

228
00:22:51,030 --> 00:22:55,710
Before you review or revise the document, you must obtain the pertinent documentation

229
00:22:55,710 --> 00:23:00,510
in the form of logs or laboratory files.

230
00:23:00,510 --> 00:23:05,470
There may be obstacles when you try to implement a revision.

231
00:23:05,470 --> 00:23:12,270
This may be because of a lack of equipment at the facility or a lack of ability to communicate

232
00:23:12,270 --> 00:23:18,560
the SOP across the laboratory users.

233
00:23:18,560 --> 00:23:24,560
And there are some solutions which may be implemented routinely for reviewing and revising

234
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:25,560
SOPs.

235
00:23:30,280 --> 00:23:37,710
We now move on to the general format of the SOP or the standard operating procedure.

236
00:23:37,710 --> 00:23:40,910
This is the general format of the SOP.

237
00:23:40,910 --> 00:23:47,550
I have not gone into specifics of SOPs as you will be developing a range of SOPs based

238
00:23:47,550 --> 00:23:50,770
on your specific organization requirements.

239
00:23:50,770 --> 00:23:53,030
These are some of the components.

240
00:23:53,030 --> 00:23:57,550
So an SOP always commences with the title page.

241
00:23:57,550 --> 00:24:06,750
The title page may include the specific logos of your organization as well as a note indicating

242
00:24:06,750 --> 00:24:10,910
that it is a controlled document and the revision number.

243
00:24:10,910 --> 00:24:22,350
SOPs which are more than three pages long must be appended with a table of contents.

244
00:24:22,350 --> 00:24:27,970
Examples of specific words or terms which may not be comprehensible to the end user

245
00:24:27,970 --> 00:24:32,070
must be included in the SOP.

246
00:24:32,070 --> 00:24:37,990
The purpose of an SOP must be stated from a regulatory context.

247
00:24:37,990 --> 00:24:45,030
For instance, SOP for the donning and doffing of personal protective equipment must clearly

248
00:24:45,030 --> 00:24:51,310
state that this SOP has been designed and developed for laboratory workers who wish or

249
00:24:51,310 --> 00:24:57,550
intend to don and doff personal protective equipment.

250
00:24:57,550 --> 00:25:03,930
The procedures in an SOP must be clearly stated in a step-by-step format.

251
00:25:03,930 --> 00:25:07,470
The health and safety warnings must be included in SOP.

252
00:25:07,470 --> 00:25:14,590
For instance, usage of a specific chemical agent for sterilization may pose certain

253
00:25:14,590 --> 00:25:17,850
health and safety risks to laboratory workers.

254
00:25:17,850 --> 00:25:20,030
This must be stated in the SOP.

255
00:25:21,930 --> 00:25:27,410
We must also include what are known as cautions because sometimes SOPs are used concurrently

256
00:25:27,410 --> 00:25:32,210
with other SOPs and this can cause interferences.

257
00:25:32,210 --> 00:25:34,930
So this must be stated in the SOP.

258
00:25:34,930 --> 00:25:42,610
For instance, a laboratory SOP for spill cleanup may involve the use of chemicals.

259
00:25:42,610 --> 00:25:50,650
And this chemicals may be hazardous to human health and may also compromise the engineering

260
00:25:50,650 --> 00:25:53,970
systems by corrosion.

261
00:25:53,970 --> 00:25:59,650
So in this case, we must state the interferences over the concurrent SOPs which must be read

262
00:25:59,650 --> 00:26:06,250
together with the SOP in general.

263
00:26:06,250 --> 00:26:12,010
Quality assurance and quality control are within the scope of the QC and QA policy of the

264
00:26:12,010 --> 00:26:17,490
organization and the SOP must take this into account as well as take into account

265
00:26:17,490 --> 00:26:26,690
other regulatory requirements during its implementation.

266
00:26:26,690 --> 00:26:32,630
The SOP must also include the references, the contact lists indicating the authors and

267
00:26:32,630 --> 00:26:34,410
the bi-risk managers.

268
00:26:34,410 --> 00:26:38,890
The related SOPs must be included in the appendices.

269
00:26:38,890 --> 00:26:45,530
The distribution concerns the number of copies which are made because the SOP administrator

270
00:26:45,530 --> 00:26:54,900
decides who will obtain the copy and the location at which the SOP will be available.

271
00:26:54,900 --> 00:27:02,140
Archiving must be mentioned in the SOP as well as archiving ensures that the SOP is

272
00:27:02,140 --> 00:27:03,880
available for audit.

273
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:10,180
As a general practice, all versions of the SOP including revisions and comments must be

274
00:27:10,180 --> 00:27:18,340
maintained in the archive in order to ensure that the process of auditing an SOP is carried

275
00:27:18,340 --> 00:27:24,930
out smoothly.

276
00:27:24,930 --> 00:27:32,660
The key messages which I wanted to get across to you in this module are as follows.

277
00:27:32,660 --> 00:27:36,140
SOPs are instructional documents which ensure reproducibility.

278
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:40,020
They are designed to achieve a single outcome.

279
00:27:40,020 --> 00:27:45,740
Ideally, one specific outcome should have one specific SOP.

280
00:27:45,740 --> 00:27:53,580
For instance, the donning of a mask should be governed by one SOP, a single SOP itself.

281
00:27:53,580 --> 00:28:01,660
The donning of a complete set of personal protective equipment will be governed by another SOP.

282
00:28:01,660 --> 00:28:06,380
Effective SOP writing takes key components into account.

283
00:28:06,380 --> 00:28:14,620
When we develop an SOP, we must take into account the inputs from the respective stakeholders

284
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:22,220
and validation and evaluation of the SOP is critical to the process of continuous quality

285
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:23,220
improvement.

286
00:28:23,220 --> 00:28:32,320
That brings us to the end of this module on standard operating procedures which are a very

287
00:28:32,320 --> 00:28:34,960
important administrative control.

288
00:28:34,960 --> 00:28:41,520
Thank you very much for watching this video lecture and I wish you a pleasant learning

289
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:42,520
experience.

290
00:28:42,520 --> 00:28:43,120
Thank you.
